 product safety and then they will need  to take that to the country's  biotechnology regulator for review and  that review can sometimes take years  if it is deemed that the product is not  regulated or similar to a conventionally  bred product  which is the case for some genome edited  products  then the product would not necessarily  need to go through that process  um and i'll talk a little bit more about  that later  um but from there any product  conventionally read or gm  would need to be scaled up and  distributed via the appropriate pathways  for whatever particular system you're in  so where exactly is that line drawn  between what's considered to be a  genetically modified  or gm product or living modified  organism  um or a genome edited product  so globally regulators have taken a very  practical approach that is informed by  the 35 years experience that they have  with transgenic more gmo plants  most regulators globally did not want to  have to issue new laws  although some are doing so for example  in india  new laws can be really difficult and  take time to move forward so instead  they've opted to interpret their  existing walls to determine whether  genome-edited products fell within the  existing definitions of a gmo  and then determine from there whether  those products could be excluded from  their oversight and treated as a  conventional byproduct  and most of the language and existing  laws was drafted  in the late 80s or early 90s  it makes a reference to recombinant dna  technology  and a novel combination of genes  as a trigger for regulation  so with some types of genome edits like  single point mutations  um deletions and introduction of dna  sequences from sexually compatible  relatives  a lot of regulators have deemed that  this is not a novel combination of  genetic material  and so some countries had said that  these products would not be regulated by  the rules meant to provide oversight for  biotechnology  although all food is still subject to  rules around food safety  which is similar to what conventionally  bred products also have to go through  so there's also a practical another  practical reason why regulators have  chosen this approach  um if something could have occurred  naturally or by conventional breeding  it's really impossible to tell which  products are genome edited in order to  require or ask for more data  so regulators are also thinking about  enforcement and also just a general  point of regulations which is for safety  and if you can't tell the difference  then there's really no point in  so as you can see from this map most of  the countries in the americas have taken  this approach  and several countries in asia and africa  are considering similar approaches  japan has taken a bit more of a  conservative approach where they're  saying if dna if any dna is introduced  so even if it's from a sexually  compatible relative for instance they're  considering that to be regulated  the one outlier  was the eu  where a court case determined that  genome-edited products fell into their  definition as a gmo as was written in  law  but earlier this year they have said  that the law as written is not fit for  purpose  and they're likely going to go about  updating that language  hopefully with more flexible language to  allow them to better adapt to new  technologies in the future  some countries haven't said anything yet  and a lot of these countries are  treating genome-edited products  as they are gm until they can establish  their laws  so just to recap  if you are considered to have a  transgenic product you may require  multi-location field trials and regulate  and regulate it as a gmo  on the need to submit a significant  regulatory package  but if the product falls into a category  of an excluded genome-edited product  it may just be treated as conventionally  spread and can move forward in the  pipeline potentially saving the  developer a lot of time and a lot of  money  and in some cases it could mean the  product getting out at all there are  examples where due to public perception  and regulatory and political roadblocks  some products have just never been put  on the market  and i think the most famous example is  the transgenic humanitarian golden rice  to prevent childhood blindness  so how big is this difference in cost  and time so  um  industry experts  uh  estimate that it can take about 14 13 to  14 years to commercialize a gm product  with about five years of that time taken  up with collecting the data for the  regulatory dossier and waiting for  regulatory approvals  um  and they estimate a much reduced cost  and time for genome edited products with  about five years and 10 million dollars  so particularly if we look at a crop  like wheat  you know for which there's currently no  jam varieties on the market  you know genetic diversity for disease  resistance has been lost over time  through bottlenecks from  polyploidization and domestication so  genome editing could play a role here  perhaps in bringing in disease  resistance resistance genes from wild  relatives  or developing varieties that can adapt  to  a range of climatic conditions  and since wheat does not generate a lot  of farm revenue compared to maize  it has not really been a target for  genetic modification  since presumably industry can't really  recover the cost associated with the  research investment  but if we have  a reduced cost and shorter times then  perhaps we might see some more public  researchers or small companies looking  into different solutions  and you know is this what we're seeing  in practice  and the short answer is yes so  in 2015 argentinian regulators pioneered  the model for how to regulate new  breeding techniques  they do require a case-by-case approach  so developers need to come in to  regulators to have their products  reviewed and products that don't have a  novel combination of genetic material is  released via existing health and safety  rules for conventionally bred products  so that was now six years ago so we have  some some early data  in 2020 the key regulators in that  country published a paper summarizing  what they found and this provides an  early example of the impact of  regulations on innovations  so right now on the screen this is the  type of developers that have submitted  applications for transgenic gmo products  the first approval was made in argentina  in 1996 of a herbicide resistant soybean  and you can see from this graph that  most emissions 90 of them were made by  foreign multinational companies  so if we compare this to genome-edited  applications by developers um we see a  wider range of distribution of who have  submitted applications  um we actually have more developers with  13 different institutions for genome  edited products  compared to just 10 institutions for the  gm product developers  and this is a comparison of 23 years of  products submitted for the gmos versus  just a three year period  evaluated for the genome edited products  and down below on the second panel um we  have the types of products that have  been submitted and if you look at the  difference between gm o's and or gm  products and genome edited products  there's a much wider variety of  genome-edited products submitted and  here again fewer applications  fewer total overall number of  applications and a shorter time frame  but still a much wider variety in just  those three years  and so what this highlights to me is  what people have been saying for a long  time but not with a lot of concrete  evidence  it shows that the cost of regulation can  really shrink the number of players that  are able to operate  and even for large multinational  companies they are limited to what they  develop because they can only focus on  crops that can be the most profitable  since the regulatory burden is very high  and so overly onerous regulations can  actually end up having the opposite  effect  i think a lot of calls by civil society  organizations for increased regulations  on gm products um is aimed explicitly at  limiting the activity of large  multinational companies but the outcome  is that it's too expensive or difficult  for all but the largest and most  well-funded players working on a tiny  fraction of trades and crops  and so that leads to more consolidation  at least monocultures  and by lowering the regulatory barrier  you can see a widening field of  companies and local and public research  institutions  because you have a wider range of  institutions you're going to have a wide  range of products people have different  interests  and some of these  new products might be consumer facing  related to sustainability or climate  change  instead of solely focused on increasing  yields and profitability  so this is exciting to me i think  it portends a really interesting future  as the landscape continues to evolve  and i personally think one of the  potentially biggest beneficiaries could  be smallholder farmers  another thing that came out of the  argentinian study is that there's a big  increase in the quantity of applications  over time for genome edited products  it's rising very quickly  and so if we put this all together you  know the products are easier and faster  to develop  potentially cheaper this makes just  means they're more accessible  and this is all  particularly useful in lmic's  so that the cgiar which  is a top class research center located  in lmic's i think they operate in over  100 countries globally  have averaged um have said that the  average age of a variety in the farmer  fields in the countries in which they  operate is 12 to 25 years  compared to three to five years in the  u.s  for corn soybean and for barley in  western europe  so using these old varieties  really leaves farmers at risk for  evolving pests and diseases and a  changing climate  and it deprives them of the progress of  breeding  and so i think what is powerful about  genome editing is that it could lead to  more responsive breeding because of the  shorter turnover times  and so breeders can provide a steady  stream of new varieties to respond to  changing climatic  conditions  but to make this a reality for lmics  it's going to be really important for us  to learn from the past and to empower  local breeders to develop and use  technology themselves  so you know scientists in the cg system  local national agricultural research  institutes are really important because  they understand the local context and  the  know what the local farmers consumers  and environmental priorities are  and one of the big criticisms of gm  technology is that it was western  developed  and so i think here it's important  that this not solely be a western  technology  but the homegrown  so the last thing i want to talk about  is public perception um we can't really  ignore it i think it's a big part of why  gm products have not been widely adopted  in lmic's and a part of the negative  feedback loop of why there are high  regulatory requirements and consumer  rejection  and so this is a big concern that we as  scientists need to appreciate and try to  address where possible before products  hit the market  so to give you an example of some data  yang and hobbs in 2020 published study  on the perception of genetically  modified and genome-edited foods  they asked people to rate from one to  six  whether they thought foods produced with  a particular breeding technique that's  natural ethical and safe to eat  for this graph on the y-axis we have the  rating scale with one being not at all  safe  natural or ethical and six being  completely  and the colors are darkest blue for  crossbred plants which received  the highest ratings across the board  light blue from mutagenesis which i  think it's funny because it's considered  a conventional breeding technique but  it's rated quite lowly  the horizontal hashtags are gm or  transgenics and the  hashtags of an angle are genome edits  which received somewhat intermediary  scores here  and when we look at other similar  studies published to date we see a  similar distribution  um so these studies have measured  people's attitudes perceptions or  willingness to buy products and at what  cost  and i've grossly oversimplified the  studies here so i can compare them  roughly  um but you can see conventional breeding  techniques generally receive a highest  positive mark with the two plus signs  genome-edited products are usually  somewhere in the middle in regards to  perception  or there's mixed perception among  different countries surveyed  and then the two negative signs indicate  that consumers had a negative  association with the breeding technique  or at least  the most negative compared to the other  options with which they were presented  and so i think overall it shows that  consumers are still a little bit more  open to genome-edited products  and then with the question around  mutagenesis it really shows you how  framing and kind of preconceptions about  uh  you know a particular word can have an  effect on how individuals  um think about  think about a product  this second paper that looked at them at  um  it's from australia  and they showed that when consumers  found out that they were already eating  products of mutagenesis and it was  considered a conventional breeding  technique that they that their  perception of it improved  so what can you as a scientist do about  all of this um so in the u.s  you know the public has a fairly high  perception of scientists  and this has been relatively stable  since the 1970s  and then in addition a lot of people are  getting their news from social media for  better or worse  so you as a scientist have a direct  platform to provide information about  your work  to your friends and family who will  likely trust your opinion and i know i  frequently i'm nervous about doing this  but then i'm reminded by you know  instagram influencer dietitians in my  social circle who have no medical or  scientific training  really giving out advice to their  followers about why you know kale is bad  for you and why you should never eat it  um and i realized okay maybe i do know a  little bit more about this and so you  shouldn't be self-conscious about  engaging because you likely do have some  training  i know this is not the space if social  media is not the space in which you want  to engage because it can sometimes be a  little hostile you can still make a  difference through structured outreach  programs um at your university or local  science centers or museums  i like to say if you're going to use the  tools in the lab then you should be  invested in conveying about its safety  and usefulness  and most importantly  why the use of technologies can be  aligned to societal values  and how these new tools can really help  us reach our goals around sustainability  and climate change  so thank you so much for your time and  attention

